  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a workflow for studying urban building material stocks and their carbon implications over time. At the center is a city block mapped at fine spatial resolution, with individual buildings represented in a 30 × 30 pixel grid and stacked by year to show a long-term, high-resolution database from about 2000 to 2020. The database appears to be built by combining documentary sources, satellite observation, geospatial data, and machine learning.\n\nUsing this time-resolved building stock, the figure then illustrates two main analyses. One is inequality in per capita building material stock, shown by comparing neighborhoods with different building densities and population icons, implying unequal access to constructed material resources. The other is quantification of embodied carbon emissions associated with the building stock.\n\nOn the right, the carbon analysis links the urban buildings to upstream industrial activities, especially extraction of building materials and their production and processing, represented by mines, trucks, and factories. Overall, the image conveys an integrated framework: reconstructing detailed building material stocks across time and space, then using that information to assess both social inequality and the embodied carbon burden of urban development.",
